1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,349
Can you predict a human cell from a mouse cell
with one subtraction and one addition?

2
00:00:05,669 --> 00:00:07,320
That's the bet scGen makes,

3
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:10,683
and it's the model your package
is wiring up right now.

4
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:13,305
To write its class, you need two things:

5
00:00:13,305 --> 00:00:14,870
the idea, in one picture,

6
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:18,001
and a clear answer
to which library code it calls.

7
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:21,933
Start with the autoencoder.

8
00:00:22,096 --> 00:00:24,912
An encoder squeezes each cell into a short code.

9
00:00:25,232 --> 00:00:29,167
In your benchmark,
a cell has about a thousand gene values;

10
00:00:29,167 --> 00:00:32,101
in Josh's config, the code is fifty numbers.

11
00:00:32,421 --> 00:00:34,853
A decoder rebuilds the cell from the code.

12
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:37,166
Training means learning to rebuild well.

13
00:00:37,486 --> 00:00:40,931
Mouse and human cells train
the same network together,

14
00:00:40,931 --> 00:00:43,865
so both species land in one shared code space.

15
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:46,828
In that space, similar cells sit close together.

16
00:00:47,148 --> 00:00:49,217
That's what makes the next step possible.

17
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:55,145
Take the mouse training cells
and average their codes.

18
00:00:55,145 --> 00:00:57,087
Do the same for the human cells.

19
00:00:57,087 --> 00:01:00,071
The difference
between the two averages is delta:

20
00:01:00,071 --> 00:01:01,284
the species shift.

21
00:01:01,334 --> 00:01:06,261
Now take cells the model never trained on,
like a held-out mouse cell type.

22
00:01:06,261 --> 00:01:10,400
Encode them, add delta, decode.
Out come predicted human cells.

23
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:11,633
Pause and ponder:

24
00:01:11,633 --> 00:01:16,967
what must be true of mouse and human cells
for one arrow to work for every cell type?

25
00:01:21,134 --> 00:01:24,867
That's the assumption: one arrow,
the same for every cell type.

26
00:01:25,187 --> 00:01:29,646
If species differences depend on the cell type,
one arrow can't capture them.

27
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:32,291
That's the gap CellOT tries to close.

28
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:37,430
Now map the picture onto the class.

29
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:39,712
Setup builds the untrained network.

30
00:01:40,032 --> 00:01:44,191
Train joins mouse
and human into one table with a species label,

31
00:01:44,191 --> 00:01:48,416
fits the network,
then computes delta and keeps it on the object.

32
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:53,114
Predict encodes the test mouse cells,
adds the stored delta, decodes,

33
00:01:53,114 --> 00:01:56,343
and returns an AnnData
with the human test genes,

34
00:01:56,343 --> 00:01:57,546
in the same order,

35
00:01:57,546 --> 00:02:01,154
so evaluate can score it
against the human test cells.

36
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:06,158
Now compare the stubs.
Train computes delta but never fits the network,

37
00:02:06,158 --> 00:02:08,758
and delta vanishes when train returns.

38
00:02:08,758 --> 00:02:13,049
Predict's last line
would hand back the mouse cells untouched,

39
00:02:13,049 --> 00:02:14,710
but it never gets there:

40
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:18,591
line 288 uses names
that only exist in the atlas script.

41
00:02:18,591 --> 00:02:20,391
Those are your blanks.

42
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:24,605
Now your question:

43
00:02:24,605 --> 00:02:27,980
do you need to trace back
into the original scgen code?

44
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:32,780
First, notice that three different things
in your project are called scgen.

45
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:35,664
One: the TensorFlow model in your atlas scripts.

46
00:02:35,984 --> 00:02:40,809
Its code is a separate clone,
in a git-ignored folder inside your project,

47
00:02:40,809 --> 00:02:44,069
and it runs
in its own TensorFlow one environment.

48
00:02:44,389 --> 00:02:48,379
Josh's helper functions are copied
from those scripts, line for line.

49
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:53,205
Two: the package
that import scgen loads in Josh's environment.

50
00:02:53,525 --> 00:02:56,192
His comment says it pulls in scvi-tools.

51
00:02:56,512 --> 00:02:59,925
And we checked that package:
it has no VAEArith at all.

52
00:03:00,245 --> 00:03:03,296
If that's what loads,
setup stops right at that name.

53
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:06,518
Three: the autoencoder in your cellot folder.

54
00:03:06,838 --> 00:03:11,762
CellOT's source is copied into your repository,
with your own edits,

55
00:03:11,762 --> 00:03:17,120
and its model named scgen is a plain
autoencoder with the same mean shift.

56
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:19,573
That one produced your benchmark numbers.

57
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:24,104
Now look at Josh's config:
two layers of two hundred fifty-six,

58
00:03:24,104 --> 00:03:28,821
fifty latent dimensions,
and a learning rate of point zero zero one.

59
00:03:29,141 --> 00:03:32,170
Those three match the cellot autoencoder's spec.

60
00:03:32,490 --> 00:03:37,097
The TensorFlow model fixes its own two layers
at eight hundred units each,

61
00:03:37,097 --> 00:03:39,338
so the layer sizes would go nowhere.

62
00:03:39,658 --> 00:03:43,620
So today, Josh's class mixes all three:
helpers from the first,

63
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,890
the import from the second,
settings from the third.

64
00:03:47,210 --> 00:03:49,493
That's why it has to pick exactly one.

65
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:58,566
So here's the rule.
Your scGenModel class is an adapter:

66
00:03:58,566 --> 00:04:01,949
the only place in your package
that speaks scgen.

67
00:04:01,949 --> 00:04:06,092
Everything else speaks your contract:
setup, train, predict.

68
00:04:06,267 --> 00:04:08,592
The adapter wraps exactly one of the three.

69
00:04:08,912 --> 00:04:12,079
If the goal is to reproduce
your benchmark numbers,

70
00:04:12,079 --> 00:04:15,184
wrap the cellot autoencoder,
because it made them.

71
00:04:15,504 --> 00:04:17,283
Settle that with Josh first.

72
00:04:17,333 --> 00:04:21,783
So, to your question:
no, you don't need the original scgen code.

73
00:04:21,783 --> 00:04:23,653
For the cellot autoencoder,

74
00:04:23,653 --> 00:04:27,671
the adapter needs only a handful of calls:
load the model,

75
00:04:27,671 --> 00:04:33,765
train it, which also computes the shift,
switch it to eval mode, and transport the cells.

76
00:04:33,765 --> 00:04:38,198
Your repo already makes these calls
in predict new input dot sh.

77
00:04:38,198 --> 00:04:39,683
Start reading there.

78
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:44,116
The payoff: in tests,
a few-line fake can stand in for the library,

79
00:04:44,116 --> 00:04:46,602
so your tests never need it installed.

80
00:04:46,922 --> 00:04:50,144
That's exactly how we ran
Josh's code for this series.

81
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:51,335
Pause and ponder:

82
00:04:51,335 --> 00:04:54,744
if you later switch
to a different scGen implementation,

83
00:04:54,744 --> 00:04:57,447
how many files in your package should change?

84
00:05:01,633 --> 00:05:06,821
Recap. scGen encodes, shifts every cell
along one learned arrow, and decodes.

85
00:05:06,821 --> 00:05:10,166
Setup builds,
train learns the network and delta,

86
00:05:10,166 --> 00:05:11,966
and predict applies them.

87
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:13,996
Three things are called scgen.

88
00:05:14,316 --> 00:05:18,092
Your adapter wraps exactly one,
and it's the only doorway to it.

89
00:05:18,412 --> 00:05:22,252
The answer to the pause:
one code file changes, the adapter,

90
00:05:22,252 --> 00:05:26,028
plus its section of the config
and the dependency it needs.

91
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:30,061
Your exercise:
open each of those four calls in cellot,

92
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:32,728
and write down its inputs and outputs.

93
00:05:33,048 --> 00:05:38,424
Then write train and predict as plain comments,
step by step, before writing any code.
